{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>In the first year</b> — All the Geonim unanimously agreed with the view of the early ones, who said: Daniel erred in his reckoning, for Jerusalem was not destroyed except when Zedekiah was captured, and that was the nineteenth year of King Nebuchadnezzar, for Jeremiah the prophet said: 'after the filling of Babylon seventy years I will visit you,' and so it was when Belshazzar was killed and Cyrus became king — then he issued the decree and Israel went up from Babylon to Jerusalem, and this is the 'visiting.' Now, there remained for the ruins of Jerusalem nineteen [years], and these are the kingdom of Cyrus the Persian. And when you count the kingdom of Darius the Mede the elder, and also the kingdom of Ahasuerus, and two years of the kingdom of Darius the Persian, he being the son of Esther — then the ruins of Jerusalem reached seventy years. And they said: Daniel began to count the destruction from the time Nebuchadnezzar took Jehoiakim to Babylon, and [then] another king reigned eight years after that. Also, some say: one year of Zedekiah, and then the destruction of Jerusalem reached seventy years.\n\nAnd Judah ha-Levi used to say: One must wonder how Daniel, who was a prophet and a sage, erred in the seventy years by nineteen years?! And he said: The count of seventy years is for the kingdom of Babylon from when Jerusalem was destroyed, and then was the visiting — not as the Geonim said. And the proof is what is said in Chronicles: 'and they became servants to him and to his sons until the reign of the kingdom of Persia,' and Cyrus the Persian was the first of the kings, and there it is written: 'to fulfill the word of YHWH by the mouth of Jeremiah, until the land had enjoyed her sabbaths, all the days that it lay desolate, to fulfill seventy years.'\n\nAnd I said to him: If one counts from the destruction of Jerusalem, what reason does Scripture have to count [from] 'in the eighth year of his reign, which is the nineteenth year'? And he answered: You tell me the reason for the reckoning: 'and it came to pass at the end of forty years'?! And I said to him: We know that the prophet said concerning Cyrus: 'he shall build my city and send out my captives,' but he did not prophesy that he would build the Temple. And he [Cyrus] sent the captives and they began to build and lay the foundation of the Temple, and the adversaries of Judah prevented them from building it. And it is written [in Aramaic]: 'behold, this city shall be built, and the palace established.' And there it is written: 'to prevent the men — that this city not be built,' and they ceased by force and power. And this verse they already interpreted [as referring to] 'to fulfill the word of YHWH by the mouth of Jeremiah,' who said: 'for after the filling of Babylon seventy years I will visit you and establish my good word upon you, to restore you to this place' — and behold, the reason they returned to Jerusalem and it remained in ruins until 'the land enjoyed her sabbaths.'\n\nAnd he said to me: Who brought you into this strait? Then I answered: But is it not written in the book of Ezra [in Aramaic]: 'and it ceased until the second year of the kingdom of Darius king of Persia,' and Haggai the prophet and Zechariah son of Iddo prophesied — then Zerubbabel and Jeshua arose to build, and it is written: 'and with them were prophets of the God of Israel supporting them'? And there it is written: 'at that time they came upon them,' etc., and they wrote a letter to King Darius, and he commanded to build 'this house of God,' and they built and completed it — by virtue of these, Israel, and by virtue of Cyrus and Darius the Mede and Artaxerxes, who is Ahasuerus, for he too commanded, only he strengthened it as Cyrus did.\n\nAnd Rabbi Judah answered: All this — what will it avail you?! Then I replied to him: Behold, you concede that Zechariah prophesied in the second year of Darius the Persian — whom they say is the son of Esther — as written in his book, and there it is written: 'how long will you not have compassion on Jerusalem, which you have rejected these seventy years.' Then he was silent."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrases quoted from Ezra and Daniel are left in English translation as Ibn Ezra quotes them in Aramaic script; no Hebrew-script Aramaic is preserved since Ibn Ezra himself marks the shift by quoting the Aramaic text directly."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:20:47.784068432Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}